# 01.asm – Stack Operations

This program demonstrates how PUSH and POP work using two values: 100 and 200.

### How the program works

The program starts by storing 100 in RAX and 200 in RBX.

mov rax, 100
mov rbx, 200


The values are then pushed onto the stack.


push rax
push rbx

100 is pushed first, followed by 200. Each PUSH decreases RSP by 8 bytes. The NOP instruction does nothing and does not affect the stack.

The values are then retrieved using POP.


pop rcx
pop rdx


200 is retrieved first into RCX, followed by 100 into RDX. Each POP increases RSP by 8 bytes.

### RSP changes
Assuming the initial RSP value is X
| Instruction | RSP |
|---|---|
| Program starts | X|
| PUSH RAX | X - 8 |
| PUSH RBX | X - 16 |
| POP RCX | X - 8 |
| POP RDX | X |

### Observations

- PUSH decreases RSP by 8 bytes.
- POP increases RSP by 8 bytes.
- The stack follows LIFO (Last In, First Out).
- RCX ends up with 200, while RDX contains 100
- RBX remains 200.
- RAX initially contains 100 but changes to 60 when the program prepares to exit
- RSP returns to its original position after both POP operations.
- The program produces no screen output because it only performs stack operations before exiting.